 think that in  many senses it was the greatest and  Athens was really renowned it was you  know import-export business for them the  imported sofas they exported guys like  Solon and great you know doctors and  commanders and all sorts of  intellectuals most famous for its wisdom  and its strength military strength  economic strength all sorts of measures  that we can use for this so Socrates  says this city here that you guys are  heard of by the way my fellow citizens  who are going to be judging me  you're like a big strong fast but kind  of lazy kind of lethargic horse that is  tough to rouse tough to get moving tough  to get going tough to send in the right  direction so you know what you need for  that he doesn't say I'm the spur he says  I'm like that stinging fly that gets a  horse awake and moving along I'm the  gadfly now the gap flies are easily  crushed the gadfly is just a tiny little  speck compared to the gigantic massive  bulk of the horse and that's why it's a  good metaphor there's all sorts of other  interesting metaphors that one could use  but that may be subject for another  video I think it's more interesting when  we think about this this central idea to  look at what Socrates actually says he's  going to do I didn't put this on the  board but one of the things that  Socrates makes really clear to them is  he's not doing this by going into  politics he says that we're ready to go  into politics in any sort of serious way  because I did it in some you know  tangential ways and they almost got me  killed I would have been dead you know  before a year because the kind of guy  that I am the integrity that I hold to  be so important the you know holding out  for the truth  as opposed to just advantage man that  sort of stuff in the the morass that is  politics and he was talking about  democratic politics in Athens at that  time that will you know earn you a lot  of enemies and those enemies will get  you killed and then I wouldn't be doing  anyb